        },
        "worked_solution": "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-5t} & t{e}^{-5t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-5t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c \\\\  & ={e}^{At}\\,c \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-5t} & t{e}^{-5t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-5t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-5t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t{e}^{-5t} \\\\ {e}^{-5t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-5t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-5t},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.$",
        "difficulty": "easy",
        "source_knowledge_point_id": "21707"
      },
      {
        "dom_id": "question-335451",
        "name": "Question 5",
        "problem": "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2x(t),\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x(-2) \\\\ y(-2)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$*",
        "answer_fields_complete": true,
        "result": "Correct",
