          "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14253317/observations/step-q334780-e0d17598c973a92f8e06.html",
          "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14253317/observations/step-q334780-e0d17598c973a92f8e06.png",
          "worked_solution": "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -11 \\\\ 11 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -11 \\\\ 11 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(11t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(11t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c \\\\  & ={e}^{At}\\,c \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(11t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(11t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(11t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(11t)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.$",
          "can_dont_know": false,
          "can_skip": false,
          "question": {
            "dom_id": "step-q334780",
            "name": "Question 3",
            "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-11y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=11x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the general solution to the system of ODEs above?\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(11t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(11t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -11 \\\\ 11 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*",
            "worked_solution": "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -11 \\\\ 11 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -11 \\\\ 11 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(11t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(11t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c \\\\  & ={e}^{At}\\,c \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(11t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(11t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(11t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(11t)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.$",
            "calculator_instructions": "",
